---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { revision } from '../lib/catalog';
---

<Layout title="About">
  <div class="page-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">UNDERSTAND. THEN USE.</div>
    <h1>
      One source.
      <br />
      Two ways to learn.
    </h1>
  </div>
  <article class="about prose">
    <h2>A small reference, built carefully</h2>
    <p>
      Shell Explain pairs original Markdown explanations with an offline Go CLI
      and this static Astro website. The same validated content supplies both
      interfaces.
    </p>
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      This website is live and the source repository is public. The offline CLI
      is released as binaries, a curl installer, a Homebrew tap and a mise
      package: see <a href="/install/">how to install it</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Source code:
      <a href="https://github.com/ninegene/shell-explain">
        github.com/ninegene/shell-explain
      </a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Content revision: <code>{revision}</code>
    </p>
    <h2>Choose the right tool</h2>
    <p>
      macOS can have GNU tools installed, and Linux can use BusyBox. Choose an
      implementation explicitly on the website. The CLI can inspect executable
      paths and use bounded version probes; it cannot see aliases or functions
      inside your parent shell. Examples are not automatically executed.
    </p>
    <h2>Sources and inspiration</h2>
    <p>
      Every command page links upstream references. The prose and examples here
      are original. This project takes inspiration from{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/tldr-pages/tldr">tldr-pages</a>,{' '}
      <a href="https://explainshell.com/">ExplainShell</a>, and{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/srsudar/eg">eg</a>; it does not import their
      text.
    </p>
    <h2>Licensing</h2>
    <p>
      Original explanations and guides are licensed under{' '}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
      Credit Shell Explain contributors, link to the source and license, and
      indicate changes when sharing. Software and shell examples use the{' '}
      <a href="https://opensource.org/license/mit">MIT License</a>. Third-party
      materials retain their own licenses.
    </p>
    <h2>Contributing later</h2>
    <p>
      Once the project opens to the public, contributors will be able to propose
      commands, examples, and portability corrections through reviewed Markdown
      pull requests. The contribution process will be verified before that
      launch.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
